// WHAT THIS IS FOR: an argument whose premises are NOT in square brackets. This is the Greenspan
// case, and it was reported from use testing as broken.
//
// THIS IS THE ONE THAT ASSERTS SOMETHING FALSE, and that is why it matters more than the rest of
// the premise-conclusion work. A premise written without a title gets an auto-generated one
// ("Untitled 1") and, under Argdown's default `statementSelectionMode: with-title`, NEVER
// BECOMES A MAP NODE. So an argument standing on five premises of which one is bracketed draws
// with exactly ONE arrow into it, and the map says the argument has one reason. It has five.
// Nothing on the page says otherwise and the reader has no way to find out.
//
// Measured against Argdown's own `greenspan.argdown`: <Turnover Argument> has FIVE premises and
// none of them is bracketed, so the whole structure is invisible and the argument arrives as a
// lone box carrying its prose description.
//
// THREE SHAPES IN ONE FILE, because the fault is about the MIX rather than about bare premises:
//   <All Bare>       no premise titled          -- draws as a lone box, no arrows at all
//   <Mixed>          one of three titled        -- draws ONE arrow, and that is the lie
//   <All Titled>     every premise titled       -- the case that always worked
//
// WHAT TO SEE: all three arguments show all of their premises, in order and numbered. <Mixed>
// must not read as an argument with a single reason.
//
// IF THE DEFECT IS PRESENT: <All Bare> has nothing attached, <Mixed> has one arrow in from
// [Named premise] alone, and only <All Titled> looks right.

[Contention]: Bubbles cannot occur in the housing market.

<All Bare>: Transaction costs render bubbles unlikely.

(1) Sales in real estate incur substantial transaction costs.
(2) Where transaction costs are substantial, market turnover is low.
(3) Where turnover is low, no speculative conflagration develops.
-----
(4) Bubbles cannot occur.
    +> [Contention]

<Mixed>: Prices track fundamentals.

(1) [Named premise]: Prices in a thick market track fundamentals.
(2) The housing market is thick.
(3) A market tracking fundamentals has no bubble.
-----
(4) The housing market has no bubble.
    +> [Contention]

<All Titled>: Diversification protects the aggregate.

(1) [Local only]: Housing bubbles are local.
(2) [Aggregate safe]: A local shock does not move the aggregate.
-----
(3) [No macro risk]: Housing bubbles carry no macroeconomic risk.
    +> [Contention]
